---
// The Independence Day 2026 flagship: one line, walked.
//
// A reader holds the stage and a small ink figure walks a landscape from 1600 to
// the end of the record. The ground is India's GDP per person (Maddison to 2022
// in 2011 int-$, chained to 2026) — but the page does not say so, anywhere,
// until the reader gets there. Nothing above this line, in the title, the
// standfirst, the meta description, the caption copy or the canvas's own alt
// text, may name the series. That withholding IS the piece: the reveal — prose
// and the axes on arrival, then, on the next press, the figure looking up as the
// camera cranes to the world's mark high overhead, and on the one after that the
// full pull-back to the whole line — only works if the reader has been walking
// something unexplained.
//
// One word in the caption copy below comes close and is approved: Plassey's
// "revenue". It is historical fact about the East India Company rather than a
// description of the ground, and it is meant to be recognised in hindsight.
// Nothing else may be added to that list.
//
// AUDITED AND KEPT, 2026-08-14 (R3). R2d's approved copy puts "the treasury is
// nearly empty" in the 1991 card. It is a fact about the government's reserves
// in June 1991 rather than about the line the reader is walking — reserves were
// around US$1.2bn, roughly two weeks of imports, with 67 tonnes of gold pledged
// abroad — and it is the nearest any pre-reveal card comes to the series. Raised
// with the user in this round and closed: the line STAYS. It is the second and
// last approved exception, alongside Plassey's "revenue". Nothing may be added
// to that list of two.
//
// One moment on the stage is interactive: at 1947 the walk stops at a flagpole
// and the reader raises the flag, which is also what raises the dawn. The rope's
// hit zone (.walk-rope) is the ONE element on this page that takes a DOWN
// gesture of its own (touch-action: none) rather than leaving it to the page's
// scroll, and it exists in the tree only while the walk is held at the pole.
// R2d's hold bar was the other one; R2g retired the bar, so the exception is
// down to one element and a few seconds of the reading.
//
// R1e made the stage a film frame, and it is THEME-INDEPENDENT: its light is a
// function of the walker's year (LIGHT_STOPS in src/lib/independence/walk.ts),
// not of --tsoi-color-*, so two readers with opposite toggles see the same dusk.
// The article around it still follows the site theme.
//
// R2b gave the piece a control band under the stage and moved the year, the era
// ribbon and three buttons (back, play, forward) into it, on the page's own
// paper. R2c took that back and put two hairline chevrons ON THE FILM, in the
// bottom corners, with the year between them.
//
// R2d is the UX round and it changes four things about the reader's hands:
//
//  · The chevrons are gone. In their place is a full-width HOLD BAR along the
//    bottom edge of the film, split 33/67 so the centre of the screen is
//    forward. A chevron looks like a step and gets tapped; a bar is a thing you
//    lean on, which is the gesture this piece is made of.
//  · The year and the era ribbon move to the TOP of the frame, out from under
//    the reader's thumb — and out of the land ink into a sky ink of their own,
//    resolved against the sky at the top of the frame rather than at the caption
//    band (see --walk-stamp-text).
//  · Entry becomes a thing the reader does. The stage opens as a POSTER — the
//    1600 frame at its in-flow size, one word on the sky — and the press that
//    answers it takes the film fullscreen (.is-theatre, plus a
//    silently-catch()ed requestFullscreen). ✕ or Esc brings the page back.
//  · Two reading-flow rules: a caption may not start fading before a wall-clock
//    floor has passed, and the years after a beat are walked at a reduced pace,
//    so a fast walker cannot outrun the words.
//
// R2g finishes the sentence R2d started and takes the bar back out. The year
// went to the top of the frame in R2d and the bar spent two rounds advertising
// hold zones at the bottom edge, where nothing else on the film was happening.
// So the chrome is gone entirely and what is left is what was always underneath
// it — the STAGE'S OWN HALVES, split 33/67 at the same asymmetry, with two
// floating labels in the middle of the picture instead of a strip along its
// foot. The two arrow keys are unchanged.
//
// R2g also stops the film opening explained. The stage lands as a DARK poster —
// the 1600 landscape at a seventh of its own light, no year, no card, one word
// breathing on the sky — and the press that answers it blooms the world up to
// full over three seconds, sends a few birds across the top of the frame, brings
// the date in behind the light, and sets the walker down on a RUNWAY of flat
// ground west of 1600 so that crossing the first year is something he does
// rather than somewhere he starts. And it splits the film's home by form factor:
// a phone gets R2d's theatre, a desktop begins IN THE FRAME with a quiet ⛶ in
// the corner. See the intro section in src/lib/independence/walk.ts.
//
// Everything over the canvas is DOM, positioned in the sky and coloured from the
// same light: --walk-text / --walk-text-dim / --walk-land-text /
// --walk-land-text-dim / --walk-text-halo / --walk-vig are published onto
// .walk-stage by the engine every time they move, and the transitions below are
// what stop them stepping. There are two inks rather than one because the
// overlay sits on two different backgrounds: the captions float in the SKY,
// whose colour crosses from night to paper, and the foot furniture stands on the
// LANDMASS, which is near-black in every palette in the table. Each ink is
// resolved against the thing it is actually drawn over (see resolveInk in
// walk.ts), which is the root fix for a caption that used to dissolve into a
// mid-grey sky around 1966. --walk-foot is still the readout's opacity, and it
// is still how the year dissolves into the pull-back's x-axis.
//
// The stage is one ordinary section in normal document flow. Nothing on this
// page reads or writes scroll position, and there is no tall track, no sticky
// and no snap. A reader can leave the stage mid-walk and come back to it exactly
// where they left it.
//
// src/lib/independence/charts.ts is still on disk and still correct; this page
// no longer uses it. It is the panel-chart implementation the five-panel build
// (git: ca6fdd0) used, which later stages will either adopt or retire.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Masthead from '../components/layout/Masthead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/layout/Footer.astro';
import ShareButtons from '../components/editorial/ShareButtons.astro';

// R2m NAMES THE PIECE. It shipped through eleven build rounds as "The walk",
// which was the working title and read as one: a description of the mechanic,
// in the register of a filename. "The Walk through Midnight" is the user's, and
// it is the piece — Nehru's midnight hour, the gate the whole film is built
// around, and the only moment in four centuries the reader does something.
//
// IT PASSES THE MYSTERY RULE, which is the only test a title on this page has to
// pass. "Midnight" is a time of night and a famous speech; nothing in it names
// money, income, output, prices or a series, and a reader who meets it in a feed
// or a tab learns nothing they are meant to walk to.
//
// This is the TITLE and not the poster. The one word on the dark frame — `walk`,
// which is the start control — is deliberately untouched: it is an instruction
// the reader answers with their thumb, and a four-word title breathing on the
// sky is a caption rather than an invitation.
const TITLE = 'The Walk through Midnight';
const STANDFIRST =
  'Four and a quarter centuries of India, on foot. Walk it yourself; the ground will explain itself at the end.';

// The social card is the WISHES CARD, not a picture of the stage. A card that
// showed the walk would have to show the ground, and the ground is the thing
// this page spends four centuries not naming — an unfurled preview is a spoiler
// served to everyone who never opens the link. So the share image is the
// piece's own 1947 midnight with the flag at the top of the mast: true to the
// piece, and giving nothing away.
//
// Both cards live in public/independence/ as static assets. og.jpg is the
// 1200x630 landscape; card.png is the 1080x1350 portrait the reader can send on
// from the ending.
//
// Release day: the landscape is served as JPEG, and the reason is WhatsApp.
// The baked og.png weighed 1.2MB and WhatsApp silently drops any og:image over
// its size ceiling, so the first real shares unfurled with no picture at all.
// The JPEG is the same bake at 137KB (the grain hides what compression costs a
// dark gradient), and the changed URL doubles as a cache-bust for every
// scraper that already stored the pictureless preview. og.png stays on disk as
// the lossless master the JPEG is derived from; nothing links to it.
//
// R2l REGENERATES BOTH FROM THE FILM ITSELF. R3 drew them in a standalone HTML
// compositor — its own sky, its own hills, its own flag — which is a second
// drawing of the piece that has to be kept true to the first one by hand, and
// was already off it. They are now a screenshot of the engine's own 1947 frame
// with the reader's own gesture completed: walked to the flagpole, the flag
// hauled to the masthead, the dawn coming up behind it, and every piece of
// furniture (the year, the era ribbon, the hints, the cards, the controls) taken
// off so the frame is the film and nothing else. The greeting lockup is composed
// over it in the sign-off's own type. The flag cannot be flag-code-wrong on
// these cards by construction, because it is the engine's own drawMast.
const OG_IMAGE = '/independence/og.jpg';
// The alt is held to the same rule as everything else on this page: it
// describes the PICTURE and names no series.
const OG_IMAGE_ALT =
  'A small figure on a dark ridge raising the Indian tricolour at first light. ' +
  'Time Series of India wishes you a happy Independence Day.';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${TITLE} — Time Series of India`}
  description={STANDFIRST}
  og_type="article"
  og_image={OG_IMAGE}
  og_image_alt={OG_IMAGE_ALT}
>
  <Masthead />

  <main class="indy">
    <!-- The lead is ordinary article furniture in normal flow, and R2e puts the
         stage back in the page column under it: a framed deck in the article's
         own measure rather than a full-bleed band. The wrapper survives for one
         reason — it is where --walk-serif is declared. -->
    <div class="walk-frame">
      <!-- Release day: the folio the film launched without. Same convention as
           the games and the reads — it states where the piece LIVES (the play
           shelf that lists it), never where the reader came from. The address
           is top-level because the piece is a flagship, but its shelf is the
           rack's. -->
      <nav class="folio tsoi-container" aria-label="Section">
        <a href="/economy">Economy</a><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><a
          href="/economy/play">Play</a
        >
      </nav>
      <header class="tsoi-container title-block">
        <!-- Share on the kicker line's right edge — the exact placement the
             Inflation Peaks masthead taught (see .mast-top in
             components/play/InflationPeaks.astro, and the same reasoning: no
             reader toolbar here, so a whole toolbar row would hold one icon).
             It shares the PAGE, which is the right object from up here: the
             link's own preview image is the midnight card, and the card file
             itself stays the ending's gift on the end-card's share. -->
        <div class="title-top">
          <p class="kicker">INDEPENDENCE DAY 2026</p>
          <ShareButtons title={TITLE} variant="icon" align="end" />
        </div>
        <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
        <p class="standfirst">{STANDFIRST}</p>
      </header>

    <div class="tsoi-container walk-column">
    <section class="walk-stage is-poster is-intro" tabindex="0" aria-label="The Walk through Midnight. Press to begin, then hold the film itself to walk: press and hold anywhere on the wide right side to walk forward, and the narrow left side walks back. The left and right arrow keys do the same. Escape leaves the film.">
      <div class="walk-canvas">
        <canvas
          role="img"
          aria-label="A landscape of ground rising and falling across the centuries, with a small figure walking it. The picture itself moves him by hand: holding the wide right side walks him forward through the years and holding the narrow left side walks him back."
        ></canvas>
        <!-- Neither of these is painted on the canvas, and for the same reason:
             both are constant, and repainting a constant across the whole frame
             sixty times a second is the most expensive thing the stage could do.
             The grain was on the canvas first (one 'overlay' fillRect a frame)
             and measured at half the frame budget on a CPU-rastered phone stage;
             as a composited layer it costs nothing per frame. The vignette
             still takes its DEPTH from the light — --walk-vig, a property write
             on the frames the light moves and not a pass. The grain takes
             nothing from anywhere: it is one constant strength for the whole
             walk (see .walk-grain below). -->
        <div class="walk-grain" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="walk-vignette" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="walk-ui">
        <!-- The date stamp. R2c had the year and the era ribbon at the FOOT of
             the film, between the two chevrons; R2d's hold bar took that edge
             for the reader's thumb, and a readout under a thumb is a readout
             nobody can read. So it goes to the top of the frame, where a date
             stamp on documentary footage goes.

             The move changes which ink it takes. At the foot it stood on the
             LANDMASS, which is near-black in every row of the light table; at
             the top it is in open SKY, whose colour crosses from midnight to
             paper across the walk. It therefore takes a sky ink of its own,
             resolved against the sky AT THIS BOX rather than at the caption
             band a fifth of the way down the frame — see --walk-stamp-text and
             resolveInk() in walk.ts. -->
        <div class="walk-centre">
          <div class="walk-foot">
            <p class="walk-year">1600</p>
            <!-- Two stacked spans so a change of period cross-fades instead of
                 blinking; walk.ts's ERAS table owns the wording and the spans.
                 It never names the series — see the mystery rule at the top. -->
            <p class="walk-era"><span class="is-shown">Mughal rule</span><span></span></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- The deck. walk.ts's CAPTION_YEARS owns the years, and R2c changed
             the rule that gets a card on and off the sky a second time. A card
             is not the last one walked past, and its fade is no longer
             symmetric: nothing is written over the sky BEFORE the year it
             belongs to, and after it the card eases up over two years, holds,
             and then takes up to thirty years to leave — clamped so it is gone
             well before the next beat speaks. So the sky is empty between beats,
             which is what a landscape looks like when nothing is happening in
             it, and walking back takes a card off for free. The last three cards
             in this block are the reveal's two parts and R2f's one ending screen,
             all stage-driven rather than year-driven (the sign-off is a fourth
             and lives outside the block entirely, below); the Nehru card is the
             one that is not the year's to give either, because it waits for the
             flag.

             R2h puts COLOUR in the deck, and the rule is one charged phrase per
             card and never more — an <em> with a hue class, no other change to
             the mark-up and NO CHANGE AT ALL to the words. The first five cards
             stay uncoloured on purpose, so the first colour the reader meets in
             the text is the famine red. The hues, what they mean and why the
             values are a shade off the approved board's are all in the
             stylesheet, under "colour in the deck".

-->

        {/* Astro strips a JSX comment from the output and ships an HTML one.
            This block is the ROUND'S OWN WORKING and it names reserves, tolls
            and a currency figure; the page above it is four centuries of not
            naming things, and an audit note that leaks what the deck withholds
            would be a poor way to record having checked the withholding. So it
            stays exactly here, beside the cards it is about, and stays out of
            the shipped HTML. The design notes above it are HTML comments and
            remain so — they say nothing a reader could not already see.

             COPY AUDIT, 2026-08-14 (R3). Every factual claim in this deck was
             re-checked against a named source. Two cards were corrected in place
             and the fix is written on each of them; four findings need an
             editorial decision rather than a correction and are recorded here
             UNFIXED, so that nothing quietly passes as checked.

             CONFIRMED, no change:
               · 1757 — Plassey, 23 June 1757 (National Army Museum). Shah Alam
                 II's farman granting the Diwani is dated 12 August 1765, so
                 "within eight years" is right to within seven weeks. Britannica,
                 "Treaty of Allahabad".
               · 1857 — the rising began at Meerut on 10 May 1857 and the sepoys
                 marched on Delhi (Britannica, "Indian Rebellion of 1857"). The
                 Government of India Act 1858 got royal assent on 2 August 1858
                 and commenced with the Queen's Proclamation on 1 November 1858.
                 "After a year of rising" is compressed — the interval is nearer
                 fifteen months — but the rising itself ran past a year, so the
                 phrase is not false and stands.
               · 1876-1902 — "some ten million" sits inside every serious range
                 and on the cautious side of the modern consensus. Colonial
                 official figures floor the three famines at ~7.6M; modern
                 demographic reconstruction ceilings them near 18M. Maharatna,
                 The Demography of Famines (1996); Davis, Late Victorian
                 Holocausts (2001), 12.2-29.3M for 1876-1902; Imperial Gazetteer
                 of India (1907).
               · 1919 — 13 April 1919, Jallianwala Bagh is the correct name of
                 the enclosed garden, firing ran about ten minutes and ~1,650
                 rounds, and the Hunter Commission's official count is 379.
                 Britannica, "Jallianwala Bagh Massacre". Note for a later round:
                 "estimates run nearly a thousand" if anything UNDERSTATES the
                 upper end — the Congress figure is ~1,000 killed and Swami
                 Shraddhanand's is 1,500.
               · 1930 — Gandhi left Sabarmati on 12 March 1930 and walked 387km /
                 240 miles to Dandi, breaking the salt law at 8:30am on 6 April
                 1930. Distance, dates and dateline all exact. Britannica,
                 "Salt March". THE LAW ITSELF, which R2m moves to the front of
                 the card, is the Salt Act of 1882 — a consolidating act that
                 reserved the manufacture and sale of salt to the government,
                 taxed what it sold, and made it an offence for anyone else to
                 make salt or collect it from a natural deposit. So "the law
                 forbids Indians to make their own salt" is the statute stated
                 rather than a paraphrase of it, and the handful Gandhi picks
                 up at Dandi is natural salt off the mud, which is exactly what
                 the act reserved.
               · 1991 — budget speech delivered 24 July 1991; the Hugo
                 attribution is Singh's own, in the speech; the quoted fragment
                 is verbatim (lower-cased mid-sentence as delivered, capitalised
                 here as a display quote, which is ordinary practice). Budget
                 1991-92 Speech of Shri Manmohan Singh, indiabudget.gov.in. The
                 "treasury is nearly empty" line is well supported: reserves were
                 about US$1.2bn in June 1991, roughly two weeks of imports, with
                 67 tonnes of gold pledged abroad.
               · 1947 — the tryst-with-destiny passage is Nehru's, to the
                 Constituent Assembly. (Strictly the delivery began late on 14
                 August and ran across midnight; "MIDNIGHT · 15 AUGUST 1947" is
                 the conventional framing and stands.)

             CORRECTED IN PLACE — see the note on each card:
               · 1947 — a silent elision inside the quotation. Fixed.
               · 1966 — "the harvests" was true of wheat and not of foodgrains.
                 Fixed to "the wheat harvest".

             FOUND WRONG, ANSWERED IN R2l. Each of the four below would have
             changed a sentence's shape, which is an editorial call rather than an
             audit's, so R3 recorded them here unfixed and reported them to the
             round's caller. THEY HAVE NOW BEEN ANSWERED: the copy session of
             2026-08-14 rewrote all four cards (and four more beside them, for
             reasons that are not the audit's), and each card carries the note of
             what it was told. The findings stay written out below as the record of
             what was wrong; the cards above are what was done about it:
               · 1600 — "the sole right to trade with Mughal India" is
                 anachronistic twice over. The 31 December 1600 charter is right,
                 but the monopoly ran over the EAST INDIES, against other English
                 subjects, and the Company had no Mughal relationship at all in
                 1600: the first voyage went to Banten on Java in 1601 and the
                 first Indian factory came at Surat in 1613. Proposed: "the sole
                 right to trade east of the Cape of Good Hope". Britannica, "East
                 India Company".
               · 1615 — Roe is correctly "England's first ambassador": he was the
                 first ACCREDITED royal ambassador, which the William Hawkins
                 precedent of 1609 does not defeat, Hawkins having travelled as a
                 Company servant. But he landed at Surat on 18 September 1615 and
                 was not received by Jahangir until 10 January 1616, so the
                 dateline "JAHANGIR'S COURT · 1615" puts him at the court a year
                 early. Proposed: "SURAT · 1615", or "JAHANGIR'S COURT · 1615-16".
               · 1690 — the card conflates two events eight years apart. On 24
                 August 1690 Job Charnock landed at Sutanuti and settled: no
                 lease, no three villages. The zamindari rights over Sutanuti,
                 Gobindapur and Kalikata were acquired on 10 November 1698, by
                 Charles Eyre, from the Sabarna Roy Choudhury family. Either the
                 event moves to 1698 or the sentence becomes Charnock's landing;
                 the second keeps the beat year and the terrain, and is the one
                 to prefer if the walk's geometry matters more than the villages.
               · 1943 — "Three million people die" is Sen's figure (Poverty and
                 Famines, 1981) and is the top of the mainstream range rather
                 than its centre. Dyson & Maharatna's re-evaluation (IESHR 28(3),
                 1991) puts excess mortality nearer 2.1M. Not indefensible, but a
                 single hard number adopts the maximum. Proposed: "Some three
                 million people die."

             R2m, THREE TOUCH-UPS, and none of them is a factual correction:
               · 1600 — shortened, and the one claim that MOVED is a claim that
                 got stronger. "A handful of merchants" was rhetorical; "a few
                 hundred" is the charter's own count, which named 218
                 adventurers. Elizabeth and Akbar's title are dropped for
                 length (the card ran to six lines on a phone). The card no
                 longer states the charter's SCOPE at all — it says only that
                 one was granted "to trade" — which is a withholding rather
                 than an error: the scope is recorded here (east of the Cape of
                 Good Hope, sole among English subjects, 218 named adventurers,
                 31 December 1600) and the card keeps "the East" back so that
                 the Company's own name is what connects London to Agra. The
                 passive "are granted" is chosen for the same reason: on the
                 day it was an office issuing a licence.
               · 1690 — "the bank of the river Hooghly" rather than "the bank
                 of the Hooghly". One word, glossing a proper name; no fact
                 changes and the dateline is untouched.
               · 1876 — the sentence drops its trailing "between 1876 and 1902".
                 The span is not lost: it was already under the card, in the
                 dateline (THE FAMINE DECADES · 1876-1902), which is where a
                 date belongs on every other card in the deck. The finding above
                 stands unchanged; only the duplicate goes.
               · 1930 — the law moves to the front of the card. See the 1882
                 Salt Act note above; no fact changes.
               · 1991 — no words change at all. The charged-phrase ink extends
                 from half the quotation to the whole of it.

             The mystery rule covers all twelve cards and was re-checked with
             them: the one approved word that comes near the series is "revenue"
             in the 1757 card, and the approved second is "treasury" in the 1991
             card (see the note at the head of this file). R2l re-checked it a
             third time against the eight rewritten cards and the two words are
             still the only two: "permits and licences" and "opens its doors" name
             a regime and a border rather than a quantity, and nothing anywhere in
             the deck states an amount of anything but people.
        */}

        <div class="walk-cards" aria-live="polite">
          <!-- R2l COPY LOCK. The card opens on TWO CITIES rather than on London
               alone, which is why the dateline drops its place: the sentence is
               already standing in Agra and in London, and a single place under it
               would name the wrong one.
               POPULATION FLOOR. "A hundred million" is the conservative floor of
               every serious estimate rather than a best guess: Davis (1951) puts
               the subcontinent at 125M in 1600 and Moosvi's reconstruction nearer
               145M, and Akbar's empire held most of it.
               THE CHARTER, for the record and no longer on the card: the 31
               December 1600 monopoly ran from the Cape of Good Hope to the
               Strait of Magellan, against other ENGLISH subjects, and against
               nobody else. R2l carried that as "the sole right to trade with the
               East", which replaced the R3 audit's "sole right to trade with
               Mughal India" (anachronistic twice over — see the working note
               above). R2m keeps the fact and drops the clause; see below.

               R2m SHORTENS IT AND TAKES THE DESTINATION OUT OF IT, and the two
               are one decision. It ran to six lines on a phone and its dateline
               reached the walker's head at the trailhead, so both titles go —
               "the emperor" and "Queen Elizabeth" cost the most and carry the
               least, the sentence being about a hundred million people and a
               charter rather than about who held either.

               THE MIDDLE SENTENCE NAMES NO DESTINATION, and that is the card
               rather than a saving. "The East" must not appear anywhere before
               the Company's own name: London is a room with a few hundred men
               in it and a piece of paper, and the thing that reaches back
               across the card to Agra is the NAME, arriving last. Say where
               they are going first and the name is a label on something the
               reader has already been told.

               "ARE GRANTED" is deliberately passive and deliberately
               bureaucratic. A charter granted is a non-event — a licence issued
               in an office — and it has to read as one, because the whole card
               is the distance between what this looked like on the day and what
               it turned out to be. An active verb ("win") makes it an
               achievement and puts the drama a sentence early.

               "A FEW HUNDRED" is no longer rhetorical at all: the charter named
               218 adventurers, so the number is what the document says. Its
               actual scope stays in the audit comment below and out of the
               card. -->
          <!-- Release day: the full stop between the two cities becomes a
               semicolon, on the device pass's finding that the sentences read
               as two unrelated facts. The semicolon is the card's whole
               argument made punctuation — one moment, two cities — and it
               costs nothing the R2m trim bought. Every word is unchanged. -->
          <p class="walk-card is-beat">
            In Agra, Akbar rules a hundred million people; in London, a few hundred merchants are
            granted a royal charter to trade. They call themselves the East India Company.<small
              >31 DECEMBER 1600</small
            >
          </p>

          <!-- R2l COPY LOCK, and it answers the R3 audit's dateline finding. Roe
               landed at Surat in September 1615; his first audience with Jahangir
               was at Ajmer in January 1616, so a dateline reading "JAHANGIR'S
               COURT · 1615" put him at the court a year early. The dateline moves
               to the landing and the sentence keeps the court, where it belongs —
               as the place he is heading for and is nobody in. -->
          <p class="walk-card is-beat">
            England’s first ambassador lands at Surat to ask permission to trade. At Jahangir’s court
            he is one petitioner among many; the empire barely notices.<small>SURAT · 1615</small>
          </p>

          <!-- R2l COPY LOCK, and it answers the R3 audit's conflation finding. The
               1690 event is Job Charnock's settlement at Sutanuti in August 1690:
               no lease and no three villages. The zamindari of Sutanuti,
