             the reader's vertical scroll is untouched everywhere else. It and
             R2d's hold bar are the only two elements on this page with
             touch-action: none. -->
        <div class="walk-rope" hidden aria-hidden="true"></div>

        <!-- R2M: the two floating text links that used to sit along the bottom
             edge here — `start over` and `send someone this card` — are gone.
             Both are inside the end-card now (see .walk-endcard above), which is
             the object they were always about: one asked the reader to restart a
             piece whose last frame was a wish, and the other to download a
             picture they had never been shown. A card that carries the wish, the
             picture and both actions is one thing to answer rather than three
             things arranged around a frame. -->

        <!-- The way out of the theatre. Top RIGHT, because top-centre is the
             date stamp's now. Two hairline strokes rather than a glyph or an
             icon font, in the dim SKY ink with the overlay's halo, on a 44px
             target that carries the safe area with it. Esc does the same thing,
             and so does the browser's own fullscreen exit — one path out, three
             ways to ask for it. -->
        <button type="button" class="walk-exit" aria-label="leave the film" hidden>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M7.5 7.5 L16.5 16.5 M16.5 7.5 L7.5 16.5" />
          </svg>
        </button>

        <!-- …and R2g's way IN, which only exists on a desktop.
             R2d made the theatre the answer everywhere: one press and the stage
             becomes the viewport. On a phone that is right — the in-flow deck is
             a postcard and the walk is a landscape — and on a desktop it is a
             modal thrown over an article the reader was reading. So a fine
             pointer begins the walk IN THE FRAME and gets this instead: two
             hairline corner brackets in the same ink and on the same 44px target
             as the ✕, in the same corner, and never at the same time as it.
             (See `coarse` in walk.ts for how the two homes are told apart.) -->
        <button type="button" class="walk-full" aria-label="fill the screen with the film" hidden>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M10 4.5 H4.5 V10 M14 19.5 H19.5 V14" />
          </svg>
        </button>

        <!-- What a reader who cannot see the flagpole is told about it. Names no
             series; the mystery rule applies here like everywhere else. -->
        <p class="walk-live" aria-live="polite"></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Sources, and this block is on the page BEFORE the reader has walked —
         a view-source reader, a screen-reader user tabbing past the stage, and
         anyone who scrolls first all meet it ahead of the reveal. So the
         mystery rule governs it exactly as it governs the deck: nothing here
         names the ground. It is "the series" throughout. Two proper nouns are
         allowed to stand, because a source you cannot name is not a source, and
         neither of them says out loud what is being measured.

         The site's read-footer register — an h2 and an ordered list in the
         byline face — because that is what the rest of the site does under a
         piece, and a flagship that invents its own colophon is a flagship
         nobody can check.

         Reads end in NAVIGATION, not promotion: there is no folio and no next-
         thing offer under this list, because the site footer immediately below
         already does the navigating. Nothing may be added here that sells
         something. -->
    <div class="tsoi-container end">
      <!-- Collapsed by default since release morning: six long items under a
           film is a page of methodology the reader meets before they have any
           reason to care. A <details> keeps it one line — the reader who wants
           to argue with the ground opens it; everyone else scrolls one row.
           The affordance is typographic (the summary's own show/hide word,
           flipped by [open]) because the piece has no icons anywhere. -->
      <details class="refs-fold">
        <summary>
          <h2>Sources</h2>
          <span class="refs-fold-cue" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <ol class="refs">
        <li>
          <!-- The link goes to the Maddison Project's own release page rather
               than to Our World in Data's grapher, and that is the mystery rule
               reaching into a URL: the OWID slug for this series spells the
               thing out, and a reader who hovers the link before walking reads
               it off their own status bar. Groningen's page carries the same
               release, is the actual source OWID redistributes, and gives
               nothing away. -->
          The ground: the <a
            href="https://www.rug.nl/ggdc/historicaldevelopment/maddison/releases/maddison-project-database-2023"
            >Maddison Project Database</a
          >, 2023 release, via Our World in Data — the series the walk is drawn from, on one
          consistent 2011 basis end to end. India before 1870 is scholarly reconstruction rather
          than measurement, and the benchmark years are sparse that far back (1600, 1650, 1700,
          1750, then decades, then every year from 1884); the line between them is a monotone
          cubic interpolation (PCHIP) that cannot invent a hill the data does not have.
        </li>
        <li>
          The dashed last stretch, 2023&ndash;2026, is not Maddison and is not measured. It is
          estimated forward from the 2022 benchmark, one step a year, using International Monetary
          Fund <em>World Economic Outlook</em> figures net of United Nations World Population
          Prospects population growth: the WEO Update of July 2026 for 2024 onward, the WEO of
          April 2026 for 2023, and the 2024 revision of World Population Prospects throughout.
          India&rsquo;s WEO rows are fiscal years and the rest of the walk is calendar years; the
          last two years are projections rather than outturns. The rim light on that stretch is
          dashed for all of these reasons at once.
        </li>
        <li>
          The famine tolls are the cautious end of the modern range: Arup Maharatna, <em
            >The Demography of Famines</em
          > (1996) and Mike Davis, <em>Late Victorian Holocausts</em> (2001) for 1876&ndash;1902,
          and Amartya Sen, <em>Poverty and Famines</em> (1981) for Bengal in 1943 — against which
          Tim Dyson and Arup Maharatna&rsquo;s re-evaluation (<em
            >Indian Economic and Social History Review</em
          > 28(3), 1991) puts the Bengal figure lower.
        </li>
        <li>
          Jallianwala Bagh: the official count of 379 is the Hunter Commission&rsquo;s. The higher
          estimates are the Indian National Congress&rsquo;s contemporary figure.
        </li>
        <li>
          The two quotations are as delivered: Jawaharlal Nehru to the Constituent Assembly on the
          night of 14&ndash;15 August 1947, and Manmohan Singh closing the budget speech of 24 July
          1991, quoting Victor Hugo (<a href="https://www.indiabudget.gov.in/doc/bspeech/bs199192.pdf"
            >Budget 1991&ndash;92 speech</a
          >). The passage from Nehru is abridged where marked and nowhere else.
        </li>
        <li>
          Further reading, and the oldest argument in the piece: Dadabhai Naoroji, <em
            >Poverty and Un-British Rule in India</em
          > (1901).
        </li>
        </ol>
      </details>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .indy {
    padding-top: calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 3);
  }

  /* --------------------------------------------------------- the frame --- */
  /* Nothing but a box that carries one custom property now. R2b made this a flex
     column so a title strip, a stage and a control band could share one
     viewport; the band is gone, the controls are on the film, and the lead is
     ordinary article furniture in normal flow above an ordinary block. What is
     left here is --walk-serif, declared below and wanted by the overlay. */

  /* -------------------------------------------------------------- lead --- */
  /* At the Inflation Peaks measure, at every width. R2b compressed all of this
     — a smaller title, a shorter deck measure, a phone-only type scale — so the
     lead, the stage and the band would fit one 100dvh column. The frame is not
     that column any more, so the compression has no job and the piece gets its
     proper masthead back. */

  /* The folio, verbatim from the games and the reads: byline face, uppercase,
     a hairline of navigation above the masthead. padding-BLOCK rather than the
     canonical block's `padding: 4px 0`, because here the folio and the
     .tsoi-container are the same element — the shorthand was zeroing the
     container's own padding-inline, which put "Economy" on the viewport edge
     on phones (desktop hid it behind the 1280px centring). */
  .folio {
    font: var(--tsoi-text-byline);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 11px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    padding-block: 4px;
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 2);
  }
  .folio a {
    color: var(--tsoi-color-primary-text);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .folio a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .folio .dot {
    color: var(--tsoi-color-on-surface-variant);
    padding-inline: 5px;
  }

  /* Full container measure, like the peaks masthead. The block carried a
     max-width: 900px, which — co-classed with .tsoi-container's auto margins —
     made the masthead a CENTRED 900px box: invisible while nothing above it
     had an edge, and a visible misalignment the moment the folio arrived (the
     kicker started ~190px right of ECONOMY · PLAY on a 1280 desktop, and the
     share control floated mid-page instead of on the right margin). The line
     lengths were never the cap's job: the h1 is four words and the standfirst
     carries its own 62ch measure. */
  .title-block {
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 2);
  }

  /* The kicker line, now a row: the label at the left edge, the share control
     at the right — InflationPeaks' .mast-top, in this masthead's own spacing.
     The kicker keeps the row's bottom margin. */
  .title-top {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 2);
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 1.5);
  }

  .kicker {
    margin: 0;
    font: var(--tsoi-text-label-caps);
    letter-spacing: var(--tsoi-text-label-caps-tracking);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--tsoi-color-primary-text);
  }

  .title-block h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font: var(--tsoi-text-headline-xl);
    letter-spacing: var(--tsoi-text-headline-xl-tracking);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .standfirst {
    margin: calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 1.5) 0 0;
    max-width: 62ch;
    font: var(--tsoi-text-deck);
    letter-spacing: var(--tsoi-text-deck-tracking);
    color: var(--tsoi-color-on-surface-variant);
  }

  /* All that survives the R2b compression block: the rhythm, tightened a
     little on a phone, where the lead and the stage are still competing for the
     same screen even though they no longer share a fixed column. The TYPE is
     the peaks page's at every width. */
  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .indy {
      padding-top: calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 1.5);
    }

    .title-block {
      margin-bottom: calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 1.5);
    }

    .title-top {
      margin-bottom: calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 0.75);
    }
  }

  /* -------------------------------------------------------------- walk --- */
  /* R2e: THE FILM COMES BACK TO THE PAGE. R2d gave the piece a theatre — one
     press takes the stage fullscreen — and the moment that existed, the in-flow
     stage stopped needing to pretend to be one. A full-bleed band edge to edge
     is a promise of immersion the page cannot keep with a masthead over it; a
     FRAMED DECK is honest about being an object on a page, and the object is the
     way in.

     So it is Inflation Peaks' register, because that is the house answer to
     "a machine sitting in an article": the page column (.tsoi-container, the
     same measure the title above it takes), a hairline in the outline token, and
     no radius — the peaks board carries none, and a rounded film frame beside a
     square one is two answers to one question.

     The height is ~58% of the viewport, which on a phone is the 55-60vh the
     round asked for and on a desktop hits the 660px ceiling before it gets
     anywhere near uncomfortable. Floored so a short landscape phone is not a
     letterbox. There is no viewport arithmetic left in here at all: R2b through
     R2d sized this box against the masthead and the lead (--walk-lead /
     --walk-head, measured by a script on every resize), and a deck in the column
     needs to know nothing about what is above it.

     Nothing here reads or hijacks scroll. The stage is an ordinary block in
     normal document flow. */

  .walk-stage {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 58dvh;
    min-height: 420px;
    max-height: min(660px, 100dvh);
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid var(--tsoi-color-outline);
    /* The canvas paints every pixel of this box, so the box's own colour is
       what a reader sees while the script and the data are still on the wire —
       one frame on a fast machine, a visible beat on a phone. It was the 1600
       sky's amber once, from before R2g: the first PAINTED frame is now the
       poster — the landscape stopped down to a seventh of its light — and a
       bright amber slab that cuts to near-black reads as a flash, where
       near-black arriving at near-black reads as the film loading. */
    background: #131015;
    /* The whole stage is the control. `manipulation` kills the double-tap zoom
       on a stage a reader is tapping and holding, and nothing else: vertical
       panning is untouched, because the page's scroll is the reader's. */
    touch-action: manipulation;
    cursor: pointer;

    /* Fallbacks. The engine overwrites these on the first frame; they exist so
       the stage is never colourless if the data or the script fails. Two inks,
       because the overlay sits on two backgrounds: --walk-text is the caption's,
       resolved against the SKY behind the caption band, and --walk-land-text is
       the foot furniture's, resolved against the LANDMASS it stands on. */
    --walk-text: rgba(240, 228, 210, 0.85);
    --walk-text-dim: rgba(240, 228, 210, 0.45);
    --walk-land-text: rgba(240, 228, 210, 0.85);
    --walk-land-text-dim: rgba(240, 228, 210, 0.45);
    /* …and a THIRD pair, R2d's: the date stamp is in open sky at the TOP of the
       frame, which is a different colour from the sky behind the caption band a
       fifth of the way down it, so it gets its own resolution rather than
       borrowing the caption's. See resolveInk() in walk.ts. */
    --walk-stamp-text: rgba(240, 228, 210, 0.85);
    --walk-stamp-text-dim: rgba(240, 228, 210, 0.45);
    --walk-text-halo: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
    /* R3: the stamp's own halo. The overlay's shared halo is computed from the
       CAPTION's ambiguity, and the two boxes cross over at different years —
       see stampAmbiguity in walk.ts. */
    --walk-stamp-halo: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
    --walk-vig: 0.3;
  }

  /* -------------------------------------------------------- the theatre --- */
  /* Entering the walk fills the screen with it. The stage leaves normal flow and
     becomes the viewport; the script locks the body's scroll behind it and puts
     it back on the way out, and it also ASKS for real fullscreen in the same
     gesture — but that request is progressive enhancement and this rule is the
     whole of the effect if it is refused.

     100dvh rather than 100vh because a phone's browser chrome is what dvh is
     for, and a film with a URL bar sitting over its bottom edge is a film with
     its hold bar under the reader's own address bar. The min/max-height on the
     in-flow stage have to be unset here or they fight the fixed box — and so
     does R2e's frame: a hairline is what says "object on a page", and there is
     no page left to be an object on. */
  .walk-stage.is-theatre {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100vw;
    height: 100dvh;
    min-height: 0;
    max-height: none;
    border: 0;
    z-index: 200;
    /* …and the theatre takes the GESTURE too, which the in-flow stage must never
       do. `manipulation` above leaves vertical panning to the browser because
       the page's scroll belongs to the reader — but in here the script has
       locked the body's scroll and there is nothing left to pan. What that
       leaves is a bug reported twice: a thumb held on the film wanders a few
       pixels as thumbs do, the browser claims the drift as the start of a pan,
       fires pointercancel, and the hold ends on a reader who never let go. So
       the fullscreen stage claims the gesture outright. Scoped to .is-theatre
       ONLY: leave the page's own scroll alone. */
    touch-action: none;
  }

  /* One serif for the whole piece. Georgia rather than the house Playfair on
     purpose: this type is set in italics and at a weight between regular and
     bold, and the site ships neither a Playfair italic nor a Playfair 500 —
     both would be synthesised. The mockups are set in Georgia for the same
     reason. (The sign-off at the very end is the one exception, and it is set in
     the house display face precisely because it is quoting the masthead.) */
  .walk-frame {
    --walk-serif: Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;
  }

  /* Desktop: the lead is an article header with room to breathe. The stage's
     own height is the same rule at every width now — 58dvh under a 660px
     ceiling — so there is nothing here for it to override. */
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .title-block {
      margin-bottom: calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 4);
    }
  }

  /* A stage a reader taps and holds must never answer with the browser's own
     feedback: no tap-highlight flash, no text/element selection starting on
     the canvas mid-hold, no long-press callout. Set on every descendant, not
     just inherited — tap-highlight and Safari's user-select don't inherit
     reliably. */
  .walk-stage,
  .walk-stage * {
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
    -webkit-touch-callout: none;
    -webkit-user-select: none;
    user-select: none;
  }

  /* A pointer press must not leave a focus ring on the frame; a keyboard reader
     who has tabbed to it still gets one, because the two arrows do something. */
  .walk-stage:focus {
    outline: none;
  }

  /* The stage's ring, and R3 makes it TWO. Measured first, then fixed: the ring
     runs the whole perimeter of the film, and the perimeter is not one
     background. At 1600 the top edge is a bright amber sky and the bottom edge
     is near-black landmass, so the caption's ink — dark, because it was
     resolved against that bright sky — measured 3.59:1 along the top and
     1.32:1 along the foot. One ink cannot clear a border that crosses the
     horizon, and no amount of resolving fixes that, because both answers are
     correct for half the ring.

     So the indicator is a PAIR of adjacent bands in the two inks the stage
     already publishes: the land ink from the edge inward, the caption's ink
     immediately inside it. They are chosen against opposite backgrounds and
     therefore land on opposite sides of every crossover — where one washes out,
     the other is at its strongest, and the two are always high-contrast against
     EACH OTHER, which is the thing a focus indicator is actually held to.

     The second band is on .walk-ui rather than on the stage because an inset
     box-shadow paints under its own element's descendants, and the canvas is a
     descendant that covers every pixel of the stage. .walk-ui is the overlay
     layer above the canvas and has no background of its own, so a shadow inset
     into it is drawn over the picture. The outline is unaffected — outlines
     paint above descendants — which is why it can stay where it is.

     Geometry: the land band occupies 0-3px from the edge, the outline 3-5px.
     Adjacent, no gap. */
  /* Release morning adds the .is-tabbed gate on both bands, for a Chromium
     habit the feel-check caught: focusing the stage by CLICK correctly shows
     no ring, but the first arrow key afterwards flips the browser's input
     modality to keyboard and lights it anyway — so a mouse reader driving with
     the arrows watched the whole film get "highlighted". The engine stamps
     .is-tabbed only when focus was actually reached BY the keyboard (a Tab
     press; any pointer press clears it), which is the distinction
     :focus-visible was supposed to draw and doesn't. A reader who tabs to the
     film keeps both bands exactly as R3 measured them. */
  .walk-stage.is-tabbed:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--walk-text);
    outline-offset: -3px;
  }

  .walk-stage.is-tabbed:focus-visible .walk-ui {
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 3px var(--walk-land-text);
  }

  .walk-canvas {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  .walk-canvas canvas {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }

  /* Release day: while the greeting holds the stage on a narrow screen, the
     settled chart under it steps back. At a phone's size the end-card's lockup,
     wish, thumbnail and actions land on top of the whole revealed picture —
     axes, both lines and their labels — and the frame reads as commotion. The
     veil is a wash of the ending's own paper sky (the 2022 palette's skyMid)
     laid over the picture, so the chart recedes into the daylight it is
     already standing in — fading the canvas itself was tried first and let the
     stage's near-black backing bleed through, which read as mud. The DOM card
     stays at full ink above it; the ✕ or a backward press hands the picture
     back. It began as a phone rule and the desktop pass kept it: the frame is
     larger there but the greeting still reads better on a quieted ground. */
  .walk-canvas::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: #eee2c8;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    transition: opacity 600ms ease;
  }
  .walk-stage.has-sign .walk-canvas::after {
    opacity: 0.55;
  }

  /* Film grain: one 96px tile of deterministic noise, repeated, overlay-blended.
     The strength is nothing like the 0.5 a plain noise plate would take — an
     overlay blend of full-range noise at 0.5 is a snowstorm — and it used to be
     a flat 0.12, the strength the canvas version had (a noise pattern at alpha
     22/255), which is the one that reads as film rather than as dirt.

     R2h made it BREATHE WITH THE ERA — 0.08 + 0.25 × --walk-vig, published by
     the engine's palette pass — and R2i puts it back. A grain that changes
     strength as the walk goes reads as the STOCK changing under the picture,
     which is a different film rather than a darker hour of the same one; the
     constant one reads as a single roll and it is the one the feel-check kept.
     So this is a literal again and the engine publishes nothing for it.

     R2j GATES IT ON THE OPENING, and that is a different question from how
     strong it is. The strength is still the one literal below and still never
     moves during the walk. What changed is that the walk is not the whole of the
     page: the poster is a near-black frame, and a noise plate over near-black
     has nothing to sit IN — the overlay blend can only lift, so what the reader
     lands on is a dark picture with speckle over it, which is dirt on the lens
     rather than grain in the stock. The device feel-check read the first load as
     "grainy orange" and this is half of that answer (the other half is the
     poster's own palette — see POSTER_DESAT in walk.ts).

     So: zero while .is-intro is on the stage, and the engine takes that class off
     at the moment it starts the bloom, which leaves the grain arriving on one
     compositor-side opacity transition timed to the light itself. --walk-bloom-ms
     is written ONCE at init from BLOOM_MS, so the two cannot drift apart; the
     fallback is R2i's bloom length in case the write never happens. This is not a
     return of R2h's per-frame --walk-grain: nothing here is written in a frame.
     */
  .walk-grain {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: 0.12;
    transition: opacity var(--walk-bloom-ms, 2800ms) linear;
    mix-blend-mode: overlay;
    background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,');
    background-repeat: repeat;
    background-size: 96px 96px;
  }

  /* Transition-free on the way DOWN and timed to the bloom on the way up, by the
     same trick the poster's note uses: a transition is read off the state being
     transitioned TO, so this rule's `none` makes going dark a cut and the base
     rule's duration makes coming back a wash. It matters twice — the class is on
     the stage in the MARKUP so the very first paint is already at zero with
     nothing animating, and `start over` puts it back instantly rather than
     dimming the grain out over five seconds under a poster that is already up. */
  .walk-stage.is-intro .walk-grain {
    opacity: 0;
    transition: none;
  }

  /* The frame closing in. Static geometry, palette-driven depth. */
  .walk-vignette {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: radial-gradient(
      ellipse 78% 82% at 50% 46%,
      rgba(6, 4, 2, 0) 40%,
      rgba(6, 4, 2, var(--walk-vig)) 100%
    );
    transition: background 400ms linear;
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------- overlay --- */
  /* Everything the reader reads, dissolved into the sky: no boxes, no rules, no
     panel. pointer-events: none throughout, so a press on the words walks
     exactly like a press on the ground under them — except the one control that
     is a control. */

  .walk-ui {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    font-family: var(--walk-serif);
    text-shadow:
      0 0 3px var(--walk-text-halo),
      0 0 12px var(--walk-text-halo);
  }

  /* --------------------------------------------------- the date stamp --- */
  /* The year and the era ribbon, top-centre, like a date burnt into documentary
     footage. R2c had them at the FOOT of the film between two chevrons; the hold
     bar took that edge, and a readout under the reader's own thumb is a readout
     that cannot be read.

     The ink changes with the position and it has to. At the foot the row stood
     on the LANDMASS, which is near-black in every row of the light table, so it
     took --walk-land-text. Up here it is in open SKY, which crosses from
     midnight to paper over four centuries — so it takes a sky ink, and a sky ink
     resolved AT THIS BOX rather than the caption's, because the top of the frame
     and the caption band a fifth of the way down it are two different colours
     (steeply so at 1600 and at the 1947 dawn). See --walk-stamp-text.

     A step smaller than the R2c foot treatment: it is orientation rather than a
     headline, and a date stamp that shouts is a chyron. */

  /* R3 gives this box the overlay's halo recomputed at its OWN sky. .walk-ui's
     text-shadow rides the caption's ambiguity, which is the right signal for the
     caption band and the wrong one here: the horizon glow is worth a quarter of
     the pixel under the caption and nothing at all at the top of the frame, so
     the two boxes pass through the ink crossover about fifteen years apart on
     the way down (~1786) and eight on the way back up (~1965). Across those two
     windows the shared halo is at its weakest exactly where the stamp's ink is
     at its most ambiguous. Same two shadows, one variable further along. */
  .walk-centre {
    position: absolute;
    top: calc(3% + env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px));
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    text-shadow:
      0 0 3px var(--walk-stamp-halo),
      0 0 12px var(--walk-stamp-halo);
  }

  .walk-foot {
    min-width: 0;
    text-align: center;
    opacity: var(--walk-foot, 1);
    /* Not a transition: the engine publishes this continuously off the
       pull-back's own ease, and a transition on top of an eased value lags it. */
  }

  /* R2m: the stamp scales with the stage on the same one multiplier the deck
     takes (see --walk-type-k). It is the film's only permanent piece of type
     and it sat at a phone's 17px on a theatre four times the width, where it
     read as a caption somebody had forgotten to set. Phones are byte-identical:
     the multiplier is exactly 1 at the reference width. */
  .walk-year {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--tsoi-font-mono);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: calc(17px * var(--walk-type-k));
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-indent: 0.12em;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--walk-stamp-text);
    transition: color 300ms linear;
  }

  .walk-era {
    display: grid;
    margin: calc(5px * var(--walk-type-k)) 0 0;
    min-height: 1.1em;
  }

  .walk-era span {
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
    justify-self: center;
    opacity: 0;
    font-family: var(--walk-serif);
    /* …and the era sub-line with it, on the same multiplier, so the step
       between the year and the era it is in stays the ratio it was authored
       at. */
    font-size: calc(10px * var(--walk-type-k));
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-indent: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--walk-stamp-text-dim);
    transition:
      opacity 240ms ease,
      color 300ms linear;
  }

  .walk-era span.is-shown {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------- the hints --- */
  /* R2d's hold bar, in two labels and no chrome.

     The bar existed because the year lived at the foot of the film and the
     reader's thumb was already down there. R2d moved the year to the top of the
     frame in the same round and the bar spent two rounds advertising controls at
     the one edge of the picture nothing was happening at. R2g deletes it — rule,
     tick, labels, both buttons and the height variable everything else was
     clearing — and puts the instruction where the reader is looking: two
     floating words in the middle of the film, one over each zone.

     THE ZONES ARE NOT THESE. The control is the whole stage, split 33/67 by the
     engine (ZONE_SPLIT in walk.ts); these are labels for it and take no pointer
     at all, which is why the block is pointer-events: none like the rest of the
     overlay and why there is no button here to tab to. The stage's own tabindex
     and the two arrow keys are the keyboard path, unchanged.

     THE HEIGHT IS 48% AND IT IS A MEASURED NUMBER. The round asked for 55-58%,
     and on a phone that is a hair above the ground line and reads correctly —
     but the vertical fit puts the TOP of the visible ground at FIT_TOP of the
     plot, which is 0.55, and on a wide desktop frame (where the screen slope is
     gentle enough that the fit's steepness cap never bites) the land's own edge
     lands at about 54% of the stage. At 56% both labels were printed on the
     black landmass in the SKY's ink, which is the one ink chosen to be wrong
     there. 48% clears the highest the ground can reach at any width and is still
     open sky, under the fog on the horizon — so they take the caption's resolved
     ink and the overlay's halo, exactly as the round intended.

     Centred in their own zones rather than in the frame: the left one over the
     middle of the back zone, the right one over the middle of the forward one.

     They arrive when the opening bloom finishes and go on the reader's first
     press of any kind, for the life of the page. */

  .walk-hints {
    position: absolute;
    top: 48%;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .walk-hint {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    margin: 0;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-family: var(--walk-serif);
    font-size: 10px;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: 0.26em;
    text-indent: 0.26em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--walk-text);
    opacity: 0;
    transition:
      opacity 900ms ease,
      color 300ms linear;
  }

  /* The middles of the two zones: 33% wide and 67% wide, so 16.5% and 66.5%. */
  .walk-hint-back {
    left: 16.5%;
  }

  .walk-hint-fwd {
    left: 66.5%;
  }

  /* R3: the alpha was 0.55 and the ink is --walk-text, which is resolved to
     clear INK_TARGET at its OWN published alpha — so multiplying a composited
     opacity on top of it threw away most of the margin the bisection had just
     bought. Measured at 0.55 the two labels ran 2.6:1 on the 1600 afternoon.
     They are up for one moment of the reading, over a sky the caption's ink was
     resolved against a quarter of a frame above them, and there is no dim ink in
     the file that is resolved for this box — so they take the full one, and the
     quiet comes from 10px uppercase at a quarter-em of tracking rather than from
     an alpha. The transition still runs from 0; only the destination moved. */
  .walk-hint.is-shown {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* Before the reader has entered the film there is one word on the stage and
     nothing else: no deck, no colophon, and no date stamp either.

     The stamp's opacity is the engine's (--walk-foot, which carries both the
     opening's arrival and the pull-back's dissolve), and this rule is what
     covers the frames before the first one is published — the poster must never
     flash a year it is about to withhold. */
  .walk-stage.is-poster .walk-cards,
  .walk-stage.is-poster .walk-signoff,
  .walk-stage.is-poster .walk-foot {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------ poster + exit --- */
  /* The invitation, and it is one word. Roughly where the captions live, in the
     sky ink with the overlay's halo, faded up a beat after first paint so it
     arrives rather than appears. No ring, no icon, nothing that looks like a
     button: the whole stage is the target.

     R2g gives it a PULSE, and the pulse is the only thing alive on the dark
     poster: the frame behind it is the 1600 landscape at a seventh of its light
     and this is the one thing at full. A slow breath rather than a blink — three
     and a half seconds, ease-in-out, and the halo swells with the alpha so the
     word looks lit rather than switched. It runs only while the invitation is
     up, so nothing is animating for the whole of the walk.

     The animation is on .is-shown rather than on the element, so the 700ms
     arrival fade is not fighting a keyframe: the word fades up first and starts
     breathing where the fade left it. */
  @keyframes walk-poster-breathe {
    0%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0.62;
      text-shadow:
        0 0 3px var(--walk-text-halo),
        0 0 14px var(--walk-text-halo);
    }
    50% {
      opacity: 0.95;
      text-shadow:
        0 0 5px var(--walk-text-halo),
        0 0 26px var(--walk-text-halo);
    }
  }

  .walk-poster {
    position: absolute;
    top: 20%;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    margin: 0;
    text-align: center;
    font-family: var(--walk-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    /* R2m: UNCHANGED at 21px, and that is a measured decision rather than an
       omission. Going from one word to three raised the obvious question of
       whether the invitation still fits; measured with a Range rather than off
       the element (which is edge-to-edge and centred, so its own box is the
       whole stage), "begin the walk" sets to 173px inside a 358px in-flow
       phone stage. It has the air it always had, so it keeps the treatment it
       always had. */
    font-size: 21px;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-indent: 0.12em;
    color: var(--walk-text);
    opacity: 0;
    transition:
      opacity 700ms ease,
      color 300ms linear;
  }

  .walk-poster.is-shown {
    opacity: 0.92;
    animation: walk-poster-breathe 3500ms ease-in-out 700ms infinite;
  }

  .walk-poster[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* R2j's one new string, and everything about it is set to keep it under the
     word rather than beside it: small against the poster's 21, the dim sky ink
     rather than the lit one, no italic, no letter-spacing, and the same 700ms
     arrival so it comes up with the invitation instead of after it.

     It follows the poster's OWN state through the sibling combinator, which is
     what keeps one source of truth for "is the invitation up": showPoster()
     toggles .is-shown on the word and this reads it.

     R2m SHOWS IT EVERYWHERE and makes it quieter. It was gated on .is-coarse
     because only a coarse pointer's press went full screen; the entry is the
     theatre on every form factor now (see begin() in walk.ts), so a desktop
     reader is owed the same warning and the gate has nothing left to gate. And
     it drops a step in both directions the round asked for — 10px against the
     old 11, at 0.7 of the dim ink's own alpha rather than 0.92 — because a note
     shown to everybody has to sit further under the one word it explains than a
     note shown to a phone did. */
  .walk-poster-note {
    position: absolute;
    top: calc(20% + 28px);
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    margin: 0;
    text-align: center;
    font-family: var(--walk-serif);
    font-size: 10px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    color: var(--walk-text-dim);
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* The transition is declared on the LIT state only, so the note arrives over
     700ms with the word and leaves the instant the word does. That asymmetry is
     the fix rather than an oversight: the word's exit is a keyframe animation
     being removed, which is a cut, and a note that fades out over most of a
     second after it is a caption left holding an empty stage. */
  .walk-poster.is-shown + .walk-poster-note {
    opacity: 0.7;
    transition:
      opacity 700ms ease,
      color 300ms linear;
  }

  /* The decline, under the note, in the note's own whisper voice — but a
     button, with the pointer to say so. Visible only while the poster is
     offering a resume (the engine unhides it), and it fades up with the same
     grammar as the note above it. */
  .walk-poster-fresh {
    position: absolute;
    top: calc(20% + 52px);
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: block;
    width: fit-content;
    padding: 4px 10px;
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    cursor: pointer;
    /* The overlay it lives in is pointer-events: none like all the film's
       words; this is a control, exactly like the ✕ and the ⛶. */
    pointer-events: auto;
    touch-action: manipulation;
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
    z-index: 3;
    font-family: var(--walk-serif);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    color: var(--walk-text-dim);
    opacity: 0;
  }

  .walk-poster-fresh[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .walk-poster.is-shown ~ .walk-poster-fresh:not([hidden]) {
    opacity: 0.75;
    transition:
      opacity 700ms ease,
      color 300ms linear;
  }

  /* The way out, and — on a desktop — the way in. Top right, because top-centre
     is the stamp's. Two strokes on a 44px target, in the dim sky ink with the
     overlay's halo carried as a drop-shadow (text-shadow does not reach an SVG
     stroke).

     The ✕ and the ⛶ share the corner and are never up together: the engine hides
     the ⛶ the moment the theatre opens and the ✕ takes its place, so one slot
     always means "the other state is one press away". Everything below is the
     same rule for both. */
  .walk-exit,
  .walk-full {
    position: absolute;
    top: calc(env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px) + 4px);
    right: calc(env(safe-area-inset-right, 0px) + 4px);
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 44px;
    height: 44px;
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    /* R3: the stamp's DIM ink, not the caption's. These two live in the top
       right corner, which is the stamp's sky rather than the caption band's —
       and the stamp's dim is the one dim ink in the piece that is genuinely
       resolved at the dim alpha instead of being the full ink's colour
       published translucent. They were on --walk-text-dim, which is that
       shorthand AND resolved against the wrong box; measured 2.7:1 at 1600. */
    color: var(--walk-stamp-text-dim);
    cursor: pointer;
    pointer-events: auto;
    touch-action: manipulation;
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
    transition: color 300ms linear;
  }

  .walk-exit[hidden],
  .walk-full[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .walk-exit:hover,
  .walk-full:hover {
    color: var(--walk-stamp-text);
  }

  .walk-exit svg,
  .walk-full svg {
    display: block;
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
    fill: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-width: 1.2;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    /* …and the halo follows the ink to the stamp's box, for the same reason. */
    filter:
      drop-shadow(0 0 3px var(--walk-stamp-halo))
      drop-shadow(0 0 12px var(--walk-stamp-halo));
  }

  /* R3's focus-ring rule, and it is one rule for the whole stage: A RING TAKES
     THE SAME VAR ITS ELEMENT'S INK DOES. A static ring colour cannot clear four
     centuries of sky — the piece already learned that about text — and the
     var-driven inks are re-resolved every frame against the exact box each
     control sits in, which is more than the 3:1 a ring is held to needs. So the
     ✕ and the ⛶ ring in the stamp's dim ink they are drawn in, `start over` and
     the download control ring in the land ink they stand on, and the stage
     itself rings in the caption's. Nothing here is a new value. */
  .walk-exit:focus-visible,
  .walk-full:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--walk-stamp-text-dim);
    outline-offset: -6px;
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------- caption --- */
  /* One caption at a time, in the sky, stacked in the same grid cell. Stacking
     rather than swapping is what keeps the block from jumping when a two-line
     caption follows a one-line one — and it is also what makes the block's
     RECT constant, which the engine measures to keep the world mark out of the
     prose at the reveal (see WORLD_MARK_* in walk.ts).

     Two kinds of card share the cell, and they are shown by different rules:

      · The twelve BEATS are driven by the walker's distance from their year.
        The engine writes their opacity every frame, so they carry no opacity
        transition of their own — a transition on top of a per-frame value only
        lags it. Their fade is in walk.ts (see cardAlpha).
      · The reveal's two and the ending's four are STAGE-driven one-shots and
        keep the old latch and its staggered cross-fade: the outgoing caption is
        most of the way out before the incoming one starts, because two
        dissolving through each other in the same cell is unreadable for the
        whole overlap.

     THE BLOCK ITSELF NEVER MOVES, and R2f is what made that a rule.

     It used to take two classes from the engine — .is-wide for the cards after
     the walk (a wider measure) and .is-signoff for the colophon (the middle of
     the frame) — and both were set on the same call that latched the incoming
     card, which is to say WHILE THE OUTGOING ONE WAS STILL FADING. Traced: on
     the press out of reveal part one the block went 350px → 405px and the
     sentence dissolving in it re-wrapped from three lines to five under the
     reader's eye; on the press to the sign-off the block jumped from 20% of the
     stage to 50% and the last ending line slid down the frame as it went. Those
     are the two text flickers the R2f feel-check reported, and they were a
     REFLOW rather than anything to do with alpha — which is why every opacity
     ramp in the trace was already clean.

     So the geometry is a constant now and the MEASURE belongs to the card: a
     beat's title card takes 30ch inside this cell and the prose cards take 38ch,
     both centred in it, and nothing about the cell changes when the latch does.
     The colophon, which is the one thing that genuinely wanted a different place
     on the stage rather than a different measure, is not in this block at all —
     see .walk-signoff. */

  /* top: 20% rather than R2c's 16%. The date stamp moved to the top of the frame
     in R2d and the caption band has to clear it: the acceptance is 24px of sky
     between the era ribbon's baseline and the top of the tallest live card on a
     390×844 phone. */
  /* R2M: THE DECK'S TYPE SCALES WITH THE STAGE, and this is the whole of the
     mechanism.
     --walk-stage-w is the stage's own width in px, unitless, written by the
     engine's resize() and by nothing else (see resize in walk.ts). It is the
     stage rather than the viewport because the film has two homes that are
     hundreds of pixels apart at the same viewport width — the article's column
     and the theatre — and a media query would set the words for a box they are
     not in.
     THE PHONE IS UNTOUCHED, and that is arithmetic rather than an intention:
     the ease starts at exactly the reference width, so at 390 the numerator is
     zero, the clamp takes its lower bound of 1, and every number below it
     computes to the px it has always been. Above that it eases — 16px of
     caption reaches 21 (1.31x) at a 900px stage — and it is CAPPED there. The
     cap is the reason for a clamp rather than a plain calc: the famine legs
     frame 26 years across the whole stage, the cards hang at 20% of it, and
     type that kept growing on a 1600px theatre would walk the deck down into
     the ground it is describing. Everything in the deck takes the same one
     multiplier, so nothing inside a card can drift out of proportion with the
     rest of it. */
  .walk-stage {
    --walk-type-k: clamp(1, calc(1 + (var(--walk-stage-w, 390) - 390) / 1655), 1.31);
  }

  .walk-cards {
    position: absolute;
    top: 20%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    /* The CELL is measured in ch and the ch is this element's own, so the block
       grows with the words in it rather than clipping them into more lines. */
    font-size: calc(16px * var(--walk-type-k));
    display: grid;
    /* The block as a whole, not the cards in it. R2d's poster sits in the
       caption band, and 1600's card used to be a WALL card lit at full from
       first paint, so on the poster frame the invitation and the opening title
       were printed over each other. Fading the BLOCK is what fixed it without
       touching the per-frame opacity the engine writes onto each card.
       R2g removes the collision at its source — the walker starts west of 1600
       on the runway, so no card is lit until he has crossed it — and this stays
       anyway, because the poster is a frame with no words on it at all and one
       composited layer is the cheapest way to say so. */
    opacity: 1;
    transition: opacity 600ms ease;
    /* The cell is as wide as the widest caption needs (the reveal's prose); the
       title-card captions take their own narrower measure inside it. */
    width: min(44ch, 92%);
  }

  /* The colophon's own place on the stage: the middle of the frame, not the
     caption band. It is a lockup rather than a sentence and it is the only thing
     the piece ever puts in the centre of the picture, so it gets a slot of its
     own instead of dragging the caption block down to it — which is what it used
     to do, over the top of a line that had not finished fading (see the block's
     note above). */
  /* pointer-events: none on the SLOT and auto on the card, which is R2m's one
     addition here. The slot is an absolutely-positioned box in the middle of
     the picture and the picture is the control: while the card is hidden a
     press inside this box must reach the stage's own zones exactly as a press
     anywhere else does, and while it is shown the card itself takes its own
     presses (the ✕ and the two buttons) without the stage hearing them. */
  .walk-signoff {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    display: grid;
    width: min(40ch, 88%);
    opacity: 1;
    transition: opacity 600ms ease;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .walk-card {
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
    align-self: start;
    justify-self: center;
    width: min(30ch, 100%);
    margin: 0;
    text-align: center;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: calc(16px * var(--walk-type-k));
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--walk-text);
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    transition:
      opacity 220ms ease,
      visibility 0s linear 220ms,
      color 300ms linear;
  }

  /* Release day: the incoming card waits for the outgoing one. The delay used
     to be 140ms against a 220ms fade-out, so on every press through the ending
     the two sentences were printed over each other for 80ms in the same cell —
     reported as a flicker of commotion at the swap. The sky goes clean first
     (220ms), takes a short breath, and only then does the next sentence rise,
     which is the same one-voice rule the beat deck already keeps. */
  .walk-card.is-shown {
    opacity: 1;
    visibility: visible;
    transition:
      opacity 300ms ease 280ms,
      visibility 0s,
      color 300ms linear;
  }

  /* …and the one card that arrives slowly rather than promptly: the sentence
     that names the world's mark. It is shown a beat after the mark itself has
     come up alone in the frame (the beat is the engine's — MARK_WORDS_GAP_MS),
     so it needs no delay of its own; what it needs is to fade rather than to
     appear, at something nearer the reveal's pace than the deck's. */
  .walk-card.is-shown.is-late {
    transition:
      opacity 1800ms ease,
      visibility 0s,
      color 300ms linear;
  }

  /* A beat card's opacity is written by the engine every frame off the walker's
     distance from its year, so it must not be transitioned or latched here. It
     is taken out of the layout's way with visibility rather than display, so the
     grid cell keeps the height of the tallest card and the block never jumps —
     and `is-lit` is the engine's own visibility latch, flipped only as a card
     leaves or enters zero. */
  .walk-card.is-beat,
  .walk-card.is-beat.is-lit {
    transition: color 300ms linear;
  }

  .walk-card.is-beat.is-lit {
    visibility: visible;
  }

  /* The reveal's two parts and the ending screen are the only cards that are
     prose rather than a title card. A caption measure of 30ch put the reveal on
     eight lines, which ran off the sky and onto the black land where ink is not
     readable; a wider measure and a step down in size keeps the whole of it in
     the air where it belongs.

     R2f moves that wider measure from the BLOCK to the card. It is the same
     38ch it always was and it renders identically; what changes is that the cell
     around it stops resizing when the latch moves, which is what the outgoing
     card used to re-wrap in. */
  .walk-card.is-prose {
    width: min(38ch, 100%);
    font-size: calc(15px * var(--walk-type-k));
    line-height: 1.45;
  }

  /* R2f's sequential fade, and R2l turns it into a CROSS-DISSOLVE. It is the
     piece's one remaining piece of choreography that is not the camera.

     Two cards are built out of LINES rather than out of one sentence: reveal
     part one (the ground is money / what the line measures) and the ending
     screen (the 1966 fact / the climb / the distance left). R2f had them arrive
     one after another and STAY, stacking up into a paragraph. The feel-check on
     that is what this round answers: a stack is a slide, and a reader who is
     given a second sentence while the first is still on the sky reads the block
     rather than the sentence. So each line now replaces the one before it — the
     outgoing one fades out as the incoming one fades in, and at rest only the
     LAST line of a card is up.

     THE SLOT IS RESERVED AND NOTHING REFLOWS, which is R2f's flicker lesson kept:
     every line of a card is in the DOM from the moment the card is latched and
     all of them occupy the SAME grid cell, so the card's box is the tallest line
     in it and does not change when the stage does. The only thing that ever
     animates here is opacity. (Screen readers get the whole card's text once,
     when the card is shown, and hear nothing at all about the dissolves.)

     The engine owns the schedule and lights each line with .is-up (see
     LINE_UP_MS / LINE_HOLD_MS / LINE_IN_MS in walk.ts). It does NOT own the
     durations: they are here, in a custom property the engine switches by class,
     so the fades a reader sees are the CSS's and the engine only decides when
     each stage starts.

     The card's own fade still runs underneath — the two multiply, and both are
     monotonic, so the composite is too. That is the whole reason the lines are
     spans inside the card rather than cards of their own: a second card in the
     cell would be a second latch to keep in step. */
  .walk-card.is-lines {
    display: grid;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .walk-line {
    display: block;
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
    opacity: 0;
    text-wrap: balance;
    transition: opacity var(--walk-line-ms, 1800ms) ease;
  }

  .walk-line.is-up {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* A line that arrives UNDER the one before it instead of over it — the
     ending's last half-sentence (see the markup). It is a .walk-line like any
     other, so it fades and is driven exactly like any other; being nested puts
     it in ordinary flow rather than in the shared grid cell, and `display:
     block` above already starts it on a line of its own. All this adds is the
     air between the two, which is a line's worth less than a paragraph's: they
     are one thought with a turn in the middle. */
  .walk-line.is-coda {
    margin-top: 0.55em;
  }

  /* The FIRST line of a card comes up at twice the cross-dissolve's pace: it is
     arriving out of an empty slot rather than out of another sentence, over a
     frame that has just stopped moving, and it is the line that tells the reader
     what they have been looking at. The engine takes this class off the moment
     the line starts to leave, so its fade-OUT is the ordinary cross-dissolve and
     the two halves of the swap are the same length. */
  .walk-line.is-slow {
    --walk-line-ms: 3600ms;
  }

  /* …and the press that says "get on with it". The class is on the card for one
     forced reflow and no longer (see cutLines in walk.ts): it is how a press-
     driven stage change is a CUT rather than another second and a half of a fade
     the reader has just asked to skip. */
  .walk-card.is-cut .walk-line {
    transition: none;
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------- colour in the deck --- */
  /* R2h. ONE charged phrase per card and never more, in four hues with fixed
     meanings drawn out of the piece's own world: LOSS in a dried-blood red,
     DEFIANCE in a lit saffron, DAWN in the 1947 horizon gold, GROWTH in the
     flag's green — plus two colours that are not a code at all but a naming, the
     line's own saffron on the word "money" and the world track's teal on the
     world's average. The teal is used for nothing else on this stage, ever: it
     is the second series, and a second series that appears in a sentence about
     something else has stopped meaning anything.

     The first five cards are deliberately uncoloured. The first colour a reader
     of this piece ever meets in the text is the famine red at 1876, which only
     works if there is nothing before it.

     The mark-up is <em> because the emphasis is real, and the emphasis is the
     COLOUR: the card is already italic and CSS italic does not compound, so
     `inherit` on both axes says out loud that a phrase is not a second italic
     and not a bolder weight either. Alpha and halo stay the card's — the span is
     inside the element whose opacity the engine writes every frame, and the
     text-shadow comes down from .walk-ui — so a coloured phrase fades in, holds
     and leaves on exactly the same curve as the words around it.

     THE VALUES ARE MEASURED rather than authored, and they are a shade off the
     approved board's. Each one is the board's hue at the nearest lightness that
     holds 4.5:1 against its own card's sky, swept across every year the card is
     at full alpha with the vignette modelled, the way the ink sweep measures
     (see resolveInk in walk.ts). Six of the seven moved by under a tenth of a
     step. The seventh is the 1966 green and it is a genuine exception, noted
     where it is used below. */
  .walk-card em {
    font-style: inherit;
    font-weight: inherit;
  }

  /* loss — 1876, 1919, 1943. One value for all three: a hue with a fixed meaning
     that changes shade between two famines is not a code. Bengal is the binding
     case (its sky is the darkest frame in the piece AND its card is clamped to
     four years, so it is still lit as the dawn starts coming up under it). */
  /* Release day: REDDER, on the same measurement. The device pass called the
     old #d67c69 more salmon than blood, and it was — the value was the board's
     hue at the lightest lightness that cleared the bar, which spent all of its
     room on lightness and none on chroma. This is the same measurement run the
     other way: the sky behind these three cards was swept again (the real
     composited frame, canvas + grain + vignette, every year each card is lit at
     both widths — the binding background is 1876's, at a relative luminance of
     0.0234), the luminance pinned at exactly the value 4.55:1 needs against it,
     and the CHROMA taken as far as that leaves room for. C 0.116 → 0.150 in
     OKLCH, hue 33° → 31°, which is a third more colour at the same legibility.
     Not to the gamut's edge, which is #ff5556 and a warning light. */
  .walk-ink-loss {
    color: #e46e5b;
  }

  /* defiance — 1930, and the only phrase that took the board's value unchanged.
     Dandi is walked across the famine floor, which is the darkest stretch of the
     table outside Bengal itself, so a lit saffron has all the room it needs. */
  .walk-ink-defiance {
    color: #f0a050;
  }

  /* dawn — 1947, inside the quotation. Lifted toward the horizon's own light
     rather than away from it, because the sky it stands on is midnight. */
  .walk-ink-dawn {
    color: #ffc993;
  }

  /* dawn, deep — 1991, and the second measured exception in this board.
     R2l takes the green off the 1991 card (green belongs to the Green Revolution
     alone now) and puts the dawn ink on the quotation instead. The 1947 value
     above cannot be reused: it was resolved against MIDNIGHT, and on the daylight
     sky of 1991 a pale gold measures 1.0:1 across the whole window the card is
     lit for — invisible, in the same way the board's on-dark green was invisible
     at 1974. So this is the dawn's own hue at the lightness the sky under THIS
     card needs: swept over every year the card is at full alpha, with the
     vignette modelled, on both the phone theatre and the desktop frame, the
     shallowest contrast it reaches is 4.5:1 (phone, ~1996; desktop, ~1995). It
     reads as a burnt amber rather than as first light, which is the honest cost
     of putting a midnight colour on a daylight card and is the same trade the
     1966 green makes below. */
  .walk-ink-dawn-deep {
    color: #8a4500;
  }

  /* …and the exception, which is the 1966 card.
     Its window runs from the Green Revolution to about 1987, and across it the
     sky climbs from the last of the morning haze to full day — through the
     luminance where the engine's own ink gives up on cream and crosses to
     charcoal. A FIXED colour cannot follow that. The board's on-dark green
     (#7db584) is very nearly isoluminant with the 1974 sky and measures 1.0:1
     there — invisible — and the on-light green above it only reaches 2.0:1 at
     the beat itself. The only green that clears 4.5:1 across the whole window is
     this one — the same hue at two fifths of the on-light variant's lightness,
     which reads as a dark ink with a green cast rather than as the flag's green.
     The real fix is not a colour: it is either the engine crossfading the two
     growth variants the way it already crossfades its own two inks (per-frame
     work, which this round is not allowed) or a shorter window for this card. */
  /* …and release day makes it GREENER by the same trick as the famine red
     above. A dark ink on a daylight sky has its lightness fixed from the other
     side — it may not get lighter than the 4.55:1 the 1966 sky allows, which is
     what makes this value dark — but nothing was ever spending the room it had
     sideways. Swept again on the real frame: C 0.046 → 0.070, hue 151° → 145°,
     toward the flag's own 142°, at the same measured contrast. It is still a
     dark ink with a green cast rather than the flag's green, and the note above
     about what the real fix would be still stands. */
  .walk-ink-growth-deep {
    color: #092e0d;
  }

  /* the line's saffron, on the word "money", and the point of it is that it is
     the colour of the line the word has just named. */
  .walk-ink-saffron {
    color: #a8541a;
  }

  /* the world's teal, on the world's average, and nowhere else in the piece.
     WORLD_COLOUR in walk.ts is what the mark and the track are drawn in. */
  .walk-ink-world {
    color: #247672;
  }

  /* midnight — the ending's last line, on the phrase that names the night the
     reader raised the flag on. The 1947 sky itself (#101423 → #1a1a2c) is too
     dark and too grey to read as a COLOUR at text size on the ending's paper
     sky, so this is the same night lifted until the blue declares itself —
     the dawn-deep trade again, in the other direction: a dark ink with a
     midnight cast rather than the sky's literal value. The ending frame is the
     one static sky in the piece (full day, both themes — the film's sky never
     forks on theme), so one measured value is enough. */
  .walk-ink-midnight {
    color: #2a3160;
  }

  /* The sub-line, and R3 takes its alpha away for the hint's reason. It sat at
     0.65 of the card's own ink; the card's ink is --walk-text, published at
     exactly the alpha the bisection needed to clear INK_TARGET against the sky
     under THIS box, and 0.65 of that is a number nobody resolved anything for.
     Measured before: 3.1:1 at 1600, 2.9:1 on the Bengal floor. There is no
     caption-band dim resolution in the file to consume (the stamp's is the only
     dim ink that is genuinely resolved, and it is resolved against a different
     sky a fifth of the frame higher), and adding one is a second bisection per
     frame on a stage with no frame budget left. So the sub-line takes the ink
     that WAS resolved for its own background, and stays subordinate the way the
     rest of the piece's small type does: 10px, uppercase, wide tracking, under a
     16px italic line. */
  /* The dateline goes up with the sentence it dates. Same one multiplier, so the
     step between the 16px line and the 10px capitals under it is the ratio it
     has always been — a dateline that stayed at 10px under a 21px card would
     read as a footnote to it rather than as part of it. The gap scales too, for
     the same reason. */
  .walk-card small {
    display: block;
    margin-top: calc(8px * var(--walk-type-k));
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: calc(10px * var(--walk-type-k));
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* --------------------------------------------------------- sign-off --- */
  /* The last thing in the piece, and the only thing on the stage that is a
     lockup rather than a sentence: the site's masthead device — Playfair
     wordmark over a saffron rule — with the rule redrawn as the flag. Two 2px
     hairlines with 2px of nothing between them, and the nothing is the point:
     at 2026 the sky behind it is TSOI paper, so the white band of the tricolour
     is the page's own colour showing through.

     Set in the house display face rather than the stage's Georgia, because it is
     quoting the masthead and a masthead quoted in the wrong face is not a
     quotation. Not italic, not a caption measure, and centred in the frame.

     R2e flipped the balance inside the lockup a step at a time and R2f inverted
     it outright, making the greeting the headline and the masthead the
     sign-under. R2l PUTS IT BACK, on the user's call: the masthead is the larger
     line again, the tricolour rule sits under it, and the greeting is the small
     quiet line beneath. The two sizes are simply swapped — 24/30px on the
     wordmark against 16/18px on the wish — and nothing else about the lockup
     moves.

     Why back: this is the site's masthead device, and a masthead is the thing
     that is set large. Read top to bottom the R2f order was a wish signed by a
     publication, which is a greeting card; this order is a publication saying
     something, which is what the whole piece has been. The wish is the last line
     on the last frame of a forty-minute walk and does not need to shout to be
     the thing the reader leaves with.

     The composition survives either way because it is a lockup and not a stack
     of type: the tricolour rule between the two lines is the axis, both lines are
     centred on the same width, and only their weights change places. */

  .walk-card.is-signoff {
    align-self: center;
    width: 100%;
    display: grid;
    justify-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    font-style: normal;
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------- the end-card --- */
  /* R2m. The lockup above is untouched; everything here is what joined it.
     The card is still a .walk-card and is still latched, faded and made visible
     by the engine exactly like every other card in the deck — so it inherits
     the deck's whole life cycle for free and there is no second latch to keep
     honest. What it adds is a box that can be pressed.

     NO PANEL AND NO ELEVATION. It is composed on the film's own sky, the way
     every other card in the piece is: the only geometry it has is the ✕ in its
     corner and the row of two actions under the picture. A boxed dialogue at the
     end of a film that has spent four centuries putting nothing between the
     reader and the frame would be the one piece of chrome in it. */
  .walk-endcard {
    position: relative;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* The card is as wide as the widest thing IN it rather than as wide as the
       slot it sits in. The slot is min(40ch, 88%) of the stage — sized for a
       caption block — and the lockup is a good deal narrower than that, so a ✕
       hung on the slot's corner floated out over the finished chart with
       nothing of the card near it. Shrink-wrapped, the ✕ is the card's own. */
    width: fit-content;
    max-width: 100%;
    /* Release morning tried a hairline box here and the device pass took it
       off: a rectangle at the centre of the finished chart read as chrome, and
       the knockout (signBox in walk.ts) already gives the lockup its clearing.
       The card's edge is the emptiness around it, like every card before it. */
  }

  .walk-endcard.is-shown {
    pointer-events: auto;
    /* …and it arrives rather than appears: the deck's 220ms is a caption's
       swap, and this is the last thing the film says. Declared on the lit
       state only, so the ✕ still takes it away at the deck's own pace.
       Release day: it also WAITS for the ending screen's last sentence to
       finish leaving (220ms and a breath), same as every other incoming card —
       without the delay the greeting was rising while the sentence was still
       dissolving a band above it. */
    transition:
      opacity 900ms ease 280ms,
      visibility 0s,
      color 300ms linear;
  }

  /* Focus lands here when the card opens, and the ring would be a rectangle
     around the whole composition. The card is not a control — it is a region
     that has been brought to the reader — and the things in it that ARE
     controls carry their own focus rings. */
  .walk-endcard:focus {
    outline: none;
  }

  /* The picture the reader is being offered, at the share card's own 4:5.
     Sized in px rather than in ch because it is a picture: 76px wide reads as a
     thumbnail of something at arm's length and still shows the mast, the ridge
     and the flag at the top of it. The hairline is the only border in the piece
     and it is doing a specific job — a dark 1947 midnight composited onto a
     2026 daylight sky has no edge of its own, and a picture with no edge is a
     smudge. */
  .walk-endcard-thumb {
    display: block;
    width: 76px;
    height: auto;
    margin-top: 2px;
    border: 1px solid var(--walk-text-dim);
    opacity: 0.92;
  }

  /* Wrapping, and each label unbreakable. "Share the Independence Day card" and
     "Start over" do not both fit across a 390px phone; the choice is between
     two tidy rows and two phrases each broken mid-sentence, and the row that
     reads is the one that wraps between the controls rather than inside them. */
  .walk-endcard-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 4px 20px;
    margin-top: 2px;
  }

  /* The two actions, in the register the two retired text links had to the
     letter: 10px tracked capitals in the resolved sky ink, a 44px target, no
     rule, no box and no icon. They stand on the SKY here rather than on the
     pulled-back land the old row stood on, so they take --walk-text (the
     caption's own resolved ink) rather than --walk-land-text. */
  .walk-share,
  .walk-restart {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 4px;
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    font-family: var(--walk-serif);
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-indent: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-align: center;
    text-decoration: none;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--walk-text);
    cursor: pointer;
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
    transition: color 300ms linear;
  }

  /* Nothing to raise on hover — they are already at the resolved ink — so the
     hover state is the halo instead, which is the only thing about them that
     was not already at full. */
  .walk-share:hover,
  .walk-restart:hover,
  .walk-endcard-close:hover {
    text-shadow:
      0 0 3px var(--walk-text-halo),
      0 0 6px var(--walk-text-halo),
      0 0 12px var(--walk-text-halo);
  }

  .walk-share:focus-visible,
  .walk-restart:focus-visible,
  .walk-endcard-close:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--walk-text);
    outline-offset: -4px;
  }

  /* The ✕, and it is the film's own ✕ in a different corner: the same two
     hairline strokes on the same 44px target in the same dim sky ink. It sits
     at the top RIGHT of the card rather than of the frame, because the frame's
     top right is the theatre's exit and two ✕s in one corner would be one
     control that means two things. */
  /* Pulled IN from the card's own corner rather than hung off it. The slot is
     min(40ch, 88%) of the stage and the lockup inside it is narrower than that,
     so a ✕ at the box's true corner floats out over the finished chart with
     nothing of the card near it — a stray glyph on the picture rather than this
     card's control. */
  .walk-endcard-close {
    position: absolute;
    top: -10px;
    right: 2px;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 44px;
    height: 44px;
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    /* The caption's own resolved ink rather than the stamp's dim. The theatre's
       ✕ is dim because it stands in the top-right sky with nothing behind it;
       this one stands on the FINISHED CHART, over gridlines, an axis and two
       drawn series, and a dim glyph on a busy ground is a smudge. */
    color: var(--walk-text);
    cursor: pointer;
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
  }

  .walk-endcard-close svg {
    width: 15px;
    height: 15px;
    fill: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-width: 1.25;
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }

  /* Out of the tab order, out of the accessibility tree, out of the picture.
     It is the download the share button falls back to and is never a control
     the reader meets — see the note in the markup. */
  .walk-send {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    margin: -1px;
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .walk-endcard-thumb {
      width: 92px;
    }

    /* Column gap only. The card is clamped to the caption slot, and in the
       Independence window the share label is long enough that the two actions
       wrap into rows at EVERY width — so the shorthand 28px was landing on the
       row gap, stacked on two 44px targets, and the pair read as strewn rather
       than set. Rows keep the phone's 4px; the 28px waits for the short
       out-of-window label, which does fit beside `Start over` here. */
    .walk-endcard-actions {
      gap: 4px 28px;
    }
  }

  .signoff-mark {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--tsoi-font-headline);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--walk-text);
  }

  /* Saffron over green with the sky between them. A border pair rather than two
     elements: the gap is the element's own content box, so nothing is painted
     into the white band at all — which is why box-sizing has to be overridden
     here. The page's reset is border-box, under which a 2px height would be
     eaten by the two 2px borders and the tricolour would lose its white. */
  .signoff-rule {
    display: block;
    box-sizing: content-box;
    width: 92px;
    height: 2px;
    border-top: 2px solid #ff9933;
    border-bottom: 2px solid #138808;
  }

  .signoff-line {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--walk-serif);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.35;
    text-align: center;
    text-wrap: balance;
    color: var(--walk-text);
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .signoff-mark {
      font-size: 30px;
    }

    .signoff-rule {
      width: 120px;
    }

    .signoff-line {
      font-size: 18px;
    }
  }

  /* --------------------------------------------------------- whisper --- */
  /* The quietest thing on the stage, and since R2d the only instruction left on
     it: the offer of the rope at the flagpole. (The opening offer was a whisper
     too until R2d gave the bar its own labels and the film a poster; there is
     nothing left for it to say.) It retires permanently on the first thing the
     reader does about it and never comes back. */

  .walk-whisper {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 5%;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    margin: 0;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: 0.26em;
    text-indent: 0.26em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--walk-land-text);
    opacity: 0.45;
    transition:
      opacity 320ms ease,
      color 300ms linear;
  }

  .walk-whisper[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* Offered only at the flagpole, and only to a reader who has been standing
     there doing nothing. It starts invisible and the engine fades it in a frame
     after it enters the tree. */
  .walk-whisper-rope {
    bottom: 11%;
    opacity: 0;
  }

  .walk-whisper-rope.is-shown {
    opacity: 0.45;
  }

  /* The ending's tap whisper shares the rope's geometry and fade: it starts
     invisible and the engine adds .is-shown a frame after unhiding it. It sits
     at the rope's own height — the two are never on the stage at once. */
  .walk-whisper-tap {
    bottom: 11%;
    opacity: 0;
  }

  .walk-whisper-tap.is-shown {
    opacity: 0.45;
  }

  /* The halyard's hit zone: invisible, sized and placed over the mast by the
     engine, and in the tree only while the walk is held at the flagpole.
     touch-action: none is what lets a DOWN-DRAG be a gesture here rather than
     the browser's scroll — and it is scoped to this element, and to those few
     seconds, precisely so the page's scroll stays the reader's everywhere
     else. */
  .walk-rope {
    position: absolute;
    pointer-events: auto;
    touch-action: none;
    cursor: grab;
  }

  .walk-rope[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* Announcements for a reader who is not looking at the stage. Visually hidden
     rather than display:none, which screen readers skip. */
  .walk-live {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    margin: -1px;
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  /* R2m: the .walk-ending-controls row that used to be styled here — `start
     over` and `send someone this card`, two runs of tracked capitals along the
     bottom edge of the pulled-back frame — is gone with the markup. Both
     controls live inside the end-card now and take their register with them,
     to the letter (see .walk-share / .walk-restart above). The one thing that
     changed on the way is the INK: the row stood on the pull-back's lightened
     land and took --walk-land-text; the card floats in the sky, so they take
     the caption's own resolved --walk-text. Each is resolved against the thing
     it is actually drawn over, which is the rule everywhere else on this
     stage. */

  /* A reader who has asked for less motion gets the captions, the era ribbon,
     the hints and the whispers without their fades too; walk.ts already cuts the
     walk to one beat per press, takes the opening bloom as a cut, sends no
     birds, hides the whisper outright rather than fading it, and spawns no dust.

     The poster's breath is the one thing here that is a KEYFRAME rather than a
     transition, and it is the only animation on the page — so it is stopped
     outright rather than shortened. What is left is a word at a steady alpha,
     which is what the invitation was before R2g gave it a pulse. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .walk-card,
    .walk-card.is-shown,
    /* …and R2l's slow arrival, which is a MORE SPECIFIC selector than the one
       above it and would otherwise win here and give this reader the one fade in
       the piece that survived their preference. */
    .walk-card.is-shown.is-late,
    .walk-card.is-beat,
    .walk-line,
    .walk-cards,
    .walk-signoff,
    .walk-era span,
    .walk-whisper,
    .walk-year,
    .walk-poster,
    .walk-poster-note,
    .walk-grain,
    .walk-hint,
    .walk-exit,
    .walk-exit svg,
    .walk-full,
    .walk-full svg,
    .walk-restart,
    .walk-share,
    .walk-endcard-close,
    .walk-vignette {
      transition: none;
    }

    .walk-poster.is-shown {
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  /* The stamp's old 19px step at 900px viewport is gone: --walk-type-k does
     that job now, off the STAGE's width rather than the window's, and the two
     rules together would have compounded. The poster's step stays — it is one
     word on a frame with nothing else on it and has no cell to grow inside. */
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .walk-poster {
      font-size: 24px;
    }
  }

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------- end --- */

  /* --------------------------------------------------------- sources --- */
  /* The read-footer register, lifted from the site's own longform pages
     (economy/read/price-of-nearly-everything.astro) rather than re-invented: a
     plain h2 and an ordered list in the byline face at 13.5px, the variant ink,
     open leading, links in the saffron text token. Same numbers, same measure.

     Held to the article's own column width rather than the stage's, because it
     is article furniture and the stage is the object it is furniture for. */
  .end {
    max-width: 900px;
    padding-top: calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 6);
    padding-bottom: calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 8);
  }

  .end h2 {
    margin: 0 0 calc(var(--tsoi-spacing-unit) * 1.5);
    font: var(--tsoi-text-headline-sm);
